## 59. Period of a Circular Orbit

This example calculates the **orbital period** of a satellite in a circular orbit around Earth.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}
  \]
- **Description**: The orbital period \( T \) depends on the orbital radius \( r \) and the mass of the central body \( M \).

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Gravitational constant \( G = 6.67430 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \)
- Mass of Earth \( M = 5.972 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg} \)
- Orbital radius \( r = 7.0 \times 10^6 \, \text{m} \)

\[
T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{(7.0 \times 10^6)^3}{(6.67430 \times 10^{-11})(5.972 \times 10^{24})}} = 5.92 \times 10^3 \, \text{s} = 1.65 \, \text{hours}
\]

- **Final Period**: \( 1.65 \, \text{hours} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 0.01 \, \text{hours} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 0.6 \% \)

